\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Memphis Mafia Bar & Grill, early morning after the jukebox sabotage
\f1\i0 \strokec2 \
Agent Jones sits on the sticky floor, weeping against the jukebox when his communicator buzzes. Embarrassed, he fumbles to answer it while hastily wiping tears.\
"Jones," he croaks.\
"Status report," demands the clipped voice of his superior at the Department of Freedom.\
Jones clears his throat, straightening his posture despite being alone. "Operation Jukebox successfully executed, sir. Elvi establishments will be playing exclusively gospel music by morning."\
"Excellent. And there's been a change of plans. You're to report directly to CEO Dunhilton's office at 0900."\
Jones stiffens. "Sir? I don't answer to Dunhilton. My chain of command is\'97"\
"Your chain of command is whatever I bloody well say it is," the voice snaps. "Dunhilton has requested our best agent for a high-priority assignment. Something about the Sublime Point situation."\
Jones's tears evaporate under the heat of sudden ambition. A direct assignment from Dunhilton himself could mean promotion, recognition\'97perhaps even the chance to expose Gerry Hardman once and for all.\
"I'll be there, sir."\
As Jones rises to his feet, he notices one of his junior agents peering at him from the shadows, clearly having witnessed his emotional breakdown.\
"If you value your continued existence in three dimensions, Agent," Jones says with menacing calm, "you will forget what you saw here."\
The junior agent nods frantically. "What did I see, sir? I see nothing. Ever. I was born blind. Actually, I don't even have eyes."\
Jones straightens his tie. "Good man."\
As he strides from the bar, Jones finds himself absently humming "You'll Never Walk Alone." He stops abruptly, horrified at himself, and punches a wall to restore balance to his emotional universe.}